<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Noun Phrases - Choosing detail</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>Add only what helps</h2>
    <p>1. Name the main thing: <strong>a bird</strong>.</p>
    <p>2. Add useful adjectives: <strong>a tiny, shivering bird</strong>.</p>
    <p>3. Add a prepositional phrase if it helps: <strong>a tiny, shivering bird beneath the leaves</strong>.</p>
    <p>4. Read it in the sentence. Every detail should clearly describe the noun you mean.</p>
    <p>“The <strong>huge, ancient tree beside the church</strong> fell” gives size, age and place.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Keep the detail next to the right noun</h2>
    <p>
      “The puppy with the red collar beside the gate” may leave it unclear whether the puppy or the collar is
      beside the gate. Move the phrase, or rewrite: “The puppy beside the gate had a red collar.”
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>Identify, or help the reader picture it?</h2>
    <p>“Give me <strong>the book on the top shelf</strong>” picks one book out from others.</p>
    <p>“A <strong>tiny, shivering bird beneath the dripping leaves</strong>” asks us to picture size, movement and setting.</p>
    <p>A longer phrase is not automatically better. Precise details beat repeated or confusing ones.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>Phrase or clause?</h2>
    <p>The boy <strong>with a blue bag</strong> uses a prepositional phrase. There is no new subject and verb.</p>
    <p>The boy <strong>who carried a blue bag</strong> uses a relative clause. <em>Who carried</em> adds a subject and verb about the boy.</p>
    <p>Both add detail. Their grammar is different.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Watch the boundaries</h2>
      <p>
        In “the old house by the river,” <em>old</em> is an adjective. The whole group is a <strong>noun phrase</strong>, not an adjective.
      </p>
      <p>In “the cat on the wall <strong>jumped</strong>,” the noun phrase ends at <em>wall</em>.</p>
      <p><em>Chocolate</em> in <em>chocolate cake</em> is a noun modifier, not an adjective.</p>
      <p>If a phrase could describe the wrong noun, move it closer or rewrite the sentence.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
